            "text": "At the end of a narrow street, someone had planted tomatoes in an abandoned shopping cart. The wheels were rusted still, and one corner leaned toward the pavement, but the vines climbed upward with the confidence of things that have never been told where they are supposed to take root.\n\nEvery morning, the baker from across the road carried out a jug of water. He never claimed the garden. When asked, he said only that the plants looked thirsty. By July, other people had contributed basil, marigolds, a wooden spoon labeled MINT, and an optimistic strawberry with exactly two leaves.\n\nThe cart became a small interruption in the neighborhood\u2019s habits. People slowed beside it. Children inspected the undersides of leaves. A bus driver ate his lunch nearby, although the bench farther down was considerably more comfortable. Nobody organized these visits. The tomatoes simply offered everyone a reason to pause awhile.\n\nOne afternoon, a ripe tomato disappeared. Then another. A handwritten sign appeared: PLEASE TAKE ONE IF YOU NEED IT. Beneath that, in smaller letters, someone added: WANTING A TOMATO COUNTS. This clarification seemed important. Generosity can become strangely bureaucratic when it asks hunger to present its credentials before being welcomed.\n\nBy autumn, the vines had browned, and the cart looked ordinary again. But the street had acquired a memory of itself as somewhere things could grow. In February, when snow filled the basket, somebody tucked a packet of seeds beneath the handle. Nobody knew who. Nobody needed to know, either."
          }
